Q: create process in user session from service A: <p>The code is correct. Make sure that you include the <code>&lt;wtsapi.h&gt;</code> header file and <code>#pragma comment(lib, &quot;WtsApi32.lib&quot;)</code>, because it is important to load the DLL, otherwise you will get a linking error (<em>error LNK2019: unresolved external symbol</em>).</p>
<p>And also, duplicating the token is not necessary. And privileges setting is not required. When the process is called from the service in your user account, the default session id will be 0, i.e. the SYSTEM account, which has all the privileges which a user can have.</p>
<p>My code works properly when a process is called from a service:</p>
<pre><code>LPCWSTR path=L&quot;C:\\Windows\\System32\\notepad.exe&quot;;//change the path accordingly

PROCESS_INFORMATION pi;
STARTUPINFO si;
DWORD nsid=1;

ZeroMemory(&amp;si, sizeof(si));
si.cb = sizeof( si );
HANDLE htoken;

DWORD sessionId;

sessionId =WTSGetActiveConsoleSessionId();
WTSQueryUserToken(sessionId,&amp;htoken);
si.wShowWindow=TRUE;
if (CreateProcessAsUser(htoken, path, NULL, NULL,
                        NULL, FALSE, 0, NULL, NULL, &amp;si, &amp;pi ))
{
    /* Process has been created; work with the process and wait for it to terminate. */
    WaitForSingleObject(pi.hProcess, INFINITE);
    CloseHandle(pi.hThread);
    CloseHandle(pi.hProcess);
}

CloseHandle(htoken);
</code></pre>
<p>For a clear idea about service, refer <a href="https://www.codeproject.com/Articles/499465/Simple-Windows-Service-in-Cplusplus" rel="nofollow noreferrer">here</a>. Download the source code and the code is perfect.</p>
<p>Just insert this piece of code in the <code>ServiceWorkerThread()</code> function. This piece of code is for opening an <code>.exe</code> file (eg. File Explorer, Notepad, etc) from your service. The <code>.exe</code> file will open when you start your service from <code>services.msc</code> (Windows application).</p>

Q: How do I parse the free format address to save into the DataBase A: <p>For US addresses, this is a solved problem in 2026. Use an address parsing API that handles the ambiguity for you.</p>
<p>For example, with <a href="https://sthan.io" rel="nofollow noreferrer">sthan.io</a></p>
<pre><code>GET https://api.sthan.io/AddressParser/USA/Single/1600%20Pennsylvania%20Ave%20NW%20Washington%20DC%2020500
Authorization: Bearer sthan_test_your_api_key
</code></pre>
<p>Response:</p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>{
  &quot;result&quot;: {
    &quot;inputAddress&quot;: &quot;1600 Pennsylvania Ave NW Washington DC 20500&quot;,
    &quot;fullAddress&quot;: &quot;1600 Pennsylvania Ave NW, Washington, DC 20500&quot;,
    &quot;addressLine1&quot;: &quot;1600 Pennsylvania Ave NW&quot;,
    &quot;addressLine2&quot;: &quot;&quot;,
    &quot;addressNumber&quot;: &quot;1600&quot;,
    &quot;streetName&quot;: &quot;Pennsylvania&quot;,
    &quot;streetPreDir&quot;: &quot;&quot;,
    &quot;streetPostType&quot;: &quot;Ave&quot;,
    &quot;streetPostDir&quot;: &quot;NW&quot;,
    &quot;unitType&quot;: &quot;&quot;,
    &quot;unitNumber&quot;: &quot;&quot;,
    &quot;city&quot;: &quot;Washington&quot;,
    &quot;stateCode&quot;: &quot;DC&quot;,
    &quot;state&quot;: &quot;District of Columbia&quot;,
    &quot;zipCode&quot;: &quot;20500&quot;,
    &quot;zip4&quot;: &quot;0005&quot;,
    &quot;county&quot;: &quot;District of Columbia&quot;,
    &quot;isPostalVerified&quot;: true,
    &quot;confidence&quot;: 0.97,
    &quot;algorithmUsed&quot;: &quot;100&quot;
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>In C#:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>using var client = new HttpClient();
client.DefaultRequestHeaders.Authorization =
    new AuthenticationHeaderValue(&quot;Bearer&quot;, &quot;sthan_test_your_api_key&quot;);

var address = Uri.EscapeDataString(textArea.Text);
var response = await client.GetFromJsonAsync&lt;ApiResponse&lt;ParsedAddress&gt;&gt;(
    $&quot;https://api.sthan.io/AddressParser/USA/Single/{address}&quot;);

var parsed = response.Result;

// Save to database
record.AddressLine1 = parsed.AddressLine1;   // &quot;1600 Pennsylvania Ave NW&quot;
record.AddressLine2 = parsed.AddressLine2;   // &quot;&quot; (or unit/apt info)
record.City          = parsed.City;           // &quot;Washington&quot;
record.State         = parsed.StateCode;      // &quot;DC&quot;
record.ZipCode       = parsed.ZipCode;        // &quot;20500&quot;
record.Zip4          = parsed.Zip4;           // &quot;0005&quot;
record.County        = parsed.County;         // &quot;District of Columbia&quot;
</code></pre>
<p>The API handles all the messy edge cases: missing ZIP codes, misspelled cities, abbreviated street types (<code>St</code> vs <code>Street</code>), directionals (<code>NW</code>, <code>S</code>), unit/apartment parsing, etc. It returns a <code>confidence</code> score (0.0-1.0) so you can flag low-confidence parses for human review.</p>
<p>The other answers are right that <strong>rolling your own parser is painful</strong> but you don't have to. If you need the addresses verified against postal records (not just parsed), the same service has a verification endpoint at <code>/AddressVerification/Usa/Single/{address}</code> that adds DPV confirmation and ZIP+4.</p>

Q: How can i fix the gap whenever colliding with an object. (Using AABB collisions) A: <p>I did what Eric said. Instead of subtracting dx i made it set to the positions of the walls (edges of the walls to be more specific) There is a + 1 and - 1 because the player was getting stuck in the wall. (The player collider's width is 12 and the height is 24)</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>  Collider *temp = collider_que.next;
  while(temp != &amp;collider_que)
  {
    Collider *next = temp-&gt;next;
    if(temp != plr.myCollider &amp;&amp; temp-&gt;type == 2 &amp;&amp; isColliding(plr.myCollider, temp))
    {
      if(dx &gt; 0)
      {
        plr.myCollider-&gt;position.x = temp-&gt;position.x - 1 - plr.myCollider-&gt;size.x;
      }else{
        plr.myCollider-&gt;position.x = temp-&gt;position.x + 1 + temp-&gt;size.x;
      }
    }
    temp = next;
  }
</code></pre>
